{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "09-well-founded.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Well-founded relations and termination",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Recognize a relation with no infinite descent, prove it has none with a measure, and prove that recursive functions terminate.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Euclid's algorithm replaces $(a, b)$ by $(b, a \\bmod b)$ until $b = 0$: $(48, 18)$, $(18, 12)$, $(12, 6)$, $(6, 0)$. Why does it stop for every input, not just this one? The second number goes down at every step, since $a \\bmod b < b$, and a natural number cannot go down forever.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Well-founded\n> Read $x \\mathrel{R} y$ as \"$x$ steps to $y$\". $R$ is **well-founded** when there is no infinite chain of steps $x_0 \\mathrel{R} x_1 \\mathrel{R} x_2 \\mathrel{R} \\cdots$. On a finite set that means exactly: no cycle.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "This is what made the induction lessons work. Induction is valid along any well-founded relation: if $P$ failed somewhere, the step would point to a smaller place where it fails, and to a smaller one from there, an infinite descent. $<$ on $\\mathbb{N}$ is lesson 6's case, and \"is a part of\" lesson 7's.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let R = rel({a, b, c, d}; a->b, b->c, a->c, c->d)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "wellfounded(R)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let C = rel({x, y, z}; x->y, y->z, z->x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "wellfounded(C)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Measures",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "On a big or infinite set you cannot follow every chain. Do what the Euclid argument did: find a number that goes down at every step.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] A measure proves termination\n> If a function $m$ to the natural numbers goes down along every step ($x \\mathrel{R} y$ gives $m(y) < m(x)$), $R$ is well-founded: an infinite chain of steps would be an infinite decreasing chain of natural numbers.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "measure(R; a->3, b->2, c->1, d->0)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "measure(R; a->3, b->2, c->2, d->0)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Find a measure for the cycle $C$: numbers for $x$, $y$ and $z$ that go down along all three steps.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "measure(C; x->2, y->1, z->0)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "There is none: going round the cycle would come back to the same value, having gone down at every step. The measure has to land in the natural numbers, too. Into the integers, $0, -1, -2, \\dots$ goes down forever, and so does $1, \\frac12, \\frac14, \\dots$ in the rationals: a measure is only as good as the order it lands in.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Termination in Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lean accepts a recursive definition only when it can see that it stops. Structural recursion (on a smaller part) it checks itself; otherwise it asks for a measure with `termination_by` and, if needed, a proof that each call decreases it with `decreasing_by`. Here is Euclid's algorithm with its measure:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def gcd' (a b : Nat) : Nat :=\n  if _h : b = 0 then a else gcd' b (a % b)\ntermination_by b\ndecreasing_by exact Nat.mod_lt _ (by omega)\n\n#eval gcd' 48 18",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Some functions have no single number that goes down. Ackermann's function calls itself with $m$ smaller and $n$ anything, or with $m$ the same and $n$ smaller:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Lexicographic order\n> Pairs are compared by their first components, and by the second only when the first are equal, as words are in a dictionary. It is well-founded when both orders are, even though the second component can grow without bound whenever the first goes down.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def ack : Nat → Nat → Nat\n  | 0, n => n + 1\n  | m + 1, 0 => ack m 1\n  | m + 1, n + 1 => ack m (ack (m + 1) n)\ntermination_by m n => (m, n)\n\n#eval ack 2 3",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In every call either $m$ goes down, or $m$ stays and $n$ goes down. No single number measures it, but the pair does.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let G = rel({s, t, u, v}; s->t, t->u, u->s, u->v)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "wellfounded(G)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Is $G$ well-founded? Answer `true` or `false`.",
      "hints": [
        "Follow the arrows from $s$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let H = rel({1, 2, 3, 4, 5}; 5->3, 3->1, 4->2, 5->4, 2->1)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "measure(H; 1->0, 2->1, 3->1, 4->2, 5->2)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Does the measure $1 \\mapsto 0$, $2 \\mapsto 1$, $3 \\mapsto 1$, $4 \\mapsto 2$, $5 \\mapsto 2$ go down along every step of $H$? Answer `true` or `false`.",
      "hints": [
        "Check each arrow: the number at its head must be smaller than at its tail."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem half_lt (n : Nat) (h : 0 < n) : n / 2 < n := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Halving a positive number makes it smaller: the measure argument for binary search.",
      "leanSolution": "  omega",
      "hints": [
        "`omega` knows about division by a numeral."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!note] The engine's own termination proof\n> ChalkMath's simplifier never runs on a step budget. Every rule in its pipeline decreases a well-founded ordering on terms: proved in Lean (`pipelineOrdered`), or, for rules that hand the work to code the ordering cannot see into (commands, matrix arithmetic), checked on each output. It is the same argument as a measure, only the measure is an ordering on expression trees.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Well-founded means no infinite descent; on a finite set, no cycle. A measure into the naturals proves it, a cycle refutes it, and Lean's `termination_by` is a measure written down. Every induction in this course ran along a well-founded relation.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
